# An array element as the variable a function's VAR_OUTPUT is assigned to.
#
# Derivation: in a formal call, "output => variable" assigns the output to
# the variable after the call (ed.3 6.6.1.7.1 table 20; 6.6.1.3; ed.2
# 2.5.1.1); an element of an array is a variable addressed by its subscript
# within the declared subrange (ed.3 6.4.4.3, 7.3.1; ed.2 2.4.1.2).
# arr is the program's VAR, initially 0 and retained between cycles. Integer
# / truncates toward zero and MOD takes the sign of the dividend (table 30);
# every dividend here is positive.
#   cycle 0, i=1, x=9: q = 9/4 = 2, arr[1] = 1; q2 = 10/4 = 2, arr[3] = 2  -> 1 0 2
#   cycle 1, i=2, x=7: q = 7/4 = 1, arr[2] = 3; q2 = 8/4 = 2,  arr[3] = 0  -> 1 3 0
#   cycle 2, i=3, x=6: q = 6/4 = 1, arr[3] = 2; q2 = 7/4 = 1,  arr[3] = 3  -> 1 3 3
# The two statements run in order, so on cycle 2 the second write of arr[3]
# is the one read.
period_ms: 100
program: main
inputs: i x
outputs: q q2 e1 e2 e3
status: conformant
reference: IEC 61131-3 ed.3 6.6.1.7.1 (table 20), 6.6.1.3, 7.3.1
---
0 i=1 x=9 => q=2 q2=2 e1=1 e2=0 e3=2
1 i=2 x=7 => q=1 q2=2 e1=1 e2=3 e3=0
2 i=3 x=6 => q=1 q2=1 e1=1 e2=3 e3=3
